An equidimensional equation $x^2 y'' + a x y' + b y = 0$ has a regular singular point at $x = 0$. A regular singular point is therefore no more singular than $x = 0$ in an equidimensional equation.

For $(1 - x^2) y'' - 2 x y' + 2 y = 0$, both
$
  (q)/(p) = (-2x)/(1-x^2), quad (r)/(p) = (2)/(1-x^2)
$
diverge as $x -> plus.minus 1$, so $x = 1$ and $x = -1$ are singular points. Yet
$
  (x-1)(q)/(p) = -(2x)/(x+1), quad (x-1)^2 (r)/(p) = (2(x-1))/(x+1)
$
are analytic at $x = 1$ with values $-1$ and $0$, so $x = plus.minus 1$ are regular singular points.

By contrast, for $(1 + sqrt(x)) y'' - 2 x y' + 2 y = 0$ the ratio $(q)/(p) = (-2x)/(1 + sqrt(x))$ tends to $0$ as $x -> 0^+$, so $x = 0$ may appear ordinary; but $(q)/(p)$ has no second derivative at $x = 0$ and $(x - 0)(q)/(p) = (-2x^2)/(1 + sqrt(x))$ has no Taylor series about $x = 0$. Hence $x = 0$ is an irregular singular point.
